{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260619005928975f763459974025","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.2","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Self-review pass 3: residual risks, scope edges, and fix before handoff. Call out any remaining production-path gap, placeholder, or handoff obligation gap before returning ready-for-verification.\n\nThis is the final review pass. I need to look critically at:\n1. Production-path gaps — is there a path where the rename/restructure breaks production behavior?\n2. Placeholders — did I leave any TODO/placeholder that's load-bearing?\n3. Handoff obligation gaps — anything downstream stories depend on that I didn't set up correctly?\n\nLet me think hard about residual risks:\n\n**Risk 1: The `validAssignments()` fixture provides turn_rendering/chunk_summary_detailed assignments so the inference provider can route them. But DEFAULT_INFERENCE_ASSIGNMENTS does NOT include them.** So in PRODUCTION, if a host constructs with `inference: { call }` (no assignments) and a turn_derivation drains, composeTurnRendering would call callKind(\"turn_rendering\") → no assignment → returns \"no assignment for derivation type turn_rendering\" failure → the handler returns providerFailed → retry → eventually failTerminal. So a default-only config (no explicit assignments) would FAIL to derive turn_rendering/smooth_turn_compression via the inference path.\n\nIs this a production-path gap? Let me think. In Story 0, the production handlers (turns/internal/derive.ts) call composeTurnRendering + compressSmoothTurn through the provider. For the inference provider to handle these, it needs assignments for turn_rendering AND smooth_turn_compression. The DEFAULT_INFERENCE_ASSIGNMENTS has smooth_turn_compression but NOT turn_rendering (turn_rendering is deterministic, no default).\n\nSo a host using `inference: { call }` (defaults only) and draining turn_derivation would get:\n- composeTurnRendering → callKind(\"turn_rendering\") → no assignment → FAIL\n- (handler returns providerFailed before reaching compressSmoothTurn)\n\nThis means turn rendering derivation FAILS under a default-only inference config. Is that a Story 0 problem?\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. AC-6.3 says deterministic types are \"not invoked during derivation\" (Story 3/4 end state). In Story 0, they ARE invoked. So there's a transitional inconsistency: AC-6.3 says deterministic types aren't invoked, but Story 0's handlers DO invoke composeTurnRendering. With default-only assignments (no turn_rendering), composeTurnRendering fails.\n\nBut wait — is this actually a regression from Epic 05? In Epic 05, the inference provider HAD composeTurnRendering routing to turn_rendering, and a full config (validAssignments) provided turn_rendering. So Epic 05's production inference path worked IF the host provided all 6 assignments. Story 0 changes: default assignments cover only 4 inference types. So a host that previously provided all 6 still works. A host using DEFAULTS only (Story 0 new feature) would fail on turn_rendering.\n\nIs \"default-only config\" a supported production path in Story 0? AC-6.4: \"Inference derivation types carry a documented default provider lane and model.\" It says INFERENCE types have defaults. turn_rendering is NOT inference (it's deterministic). So defaults don't cover it. A default-only config is meant for the inference types. But the handler still calls composeTurnRendering (deterministic op) which needs an assignment.\n\nThis is a genuine transitional tension. But — is it a BLOCKER for Story 0? Let me reconsider what Story 0 actually delivers:\n- Story 0 = foundation (restructure, rename, config). It does NOT change handler behavior (scope: \"no behavioral derivation method changes beyond rename/config cleanup\").\n- The handlers still call composeTurnRendering (Epic 05 behavior).\n- AC-6.3/6.4 are about the CONFIG contract, not handler behavior.\n- TC-6.4a tests that inference types resolve to defaults — it does NOT drain turn_derivation under a default config. It calls provider ops directly.\n\nSo Story 0 doesn't CLAIM that a default-only config fully drains turn_derivation. The default config provides inference-type defaults; the deterministic ops (turn_rendering) still need explicit assignment OR are handled by the deterministic provider in production.\n\nActually — wait. In PRODUCTION (pi-lhc extension), how is the SDK constructed? Per the tech design, the host provides a ModelCall + assignments. The host would provide assignments for the types it wants routed to the model. For deterministic types, the host uses the DETERMINISTIC provider (direct injection), not the inference path. So:\n- If host uses `provider: createDeterministicProvider()` → all ops are deterministic (no model calls). turn_rendering works.\n- If host uses `inference: { call, assignments }` → model-routed. The host provides assignments for inference types; turn_rendering/chunk_summary_detailed are deterministic and... hmm, but the inference adapter DOES route composeTurnRendering.\n\nThis is the core Epic 05 design question that I shouldn't re-litigate in Story 0. The KEY point: Story 0 doesn't change this. Whatever worked in Epic 05 still works in Story 0 (the handler/provider behavior is unchanged except the rename). The default-assignment feature (AC-6.4) is NEW and provides inference-type defaults — it doesn't claim to make default-only configs fully drain deterministic ops.\n\nSo this is NOT a Story 0 regression. It's a pre-existing Epic 05 design characteristic, and the AC-6.3 \"not invoked\" resolution comes in Story 3/4. I've already surfaced this in findingsSurfaced.\n\nBUT — let me double-check there's no actual REGRESSION. In Epic 05, did `inference: { call }` (no assignments) even construct? No — Epic 05 REQUIRED all assignments (the old resolveInferenceProvider threw on missing). So Epic 05 hosts ALWAYS provided full assignments. Story 0 makes assignments optional with defaults. So Story 0's default-only config is a NEW capability that works for inference types. The deterministic ops needing assignment is unchanged (Epic 05 hosts provided them; Story 0 default-only is for inference-only scenarios).\n\nSo no regression. The TC-6.4a test (default config + direct provider calls) works because it only calls inference ops. A full drain under default-only config would hit the deterministic-op-no-assignment issue, but that's not a Story 0 obligation (and not tested because it's the Story 3/4 transition).\n\nHmm, but is this a \"production-path gap\" I should call out? Let me reconsider. The story's \"Production Path Proof\" says: \"Entrypoint: createSdk construction and thread-file open/migration. Registration/default path: SDK construction fills defaults and wires the scheduler. Evidence: assignment-config.test.ts, restructure-boundaries.test.ts, pnpm run boundaries.\" So the production path proof is about construction + defaults + scheduler wiring + migration — all of which I've covered. It does NOT require a full drain under default-only config. So I've met the production-path proof obligation.\n\nI'll surface this transitional tension more explicitly as a residual risk (it's already partially in findingsSurfaced but let me make sure it's clear that a default-only inference config won't fully drain deterministic ops until Story 3/4). Actually — I already have the AC-6.3 finding. Let me strengthen it slightly to note the production-path implication. But it's not a blocker.\n\n**Risk 2: Placeholders.** Did I leave any TODO? Let me check. The prior pass had a TODO in prompts/index.ts (\"TODO: rename to smooth-turn-compression-v1\") which I REMOVED. Let me grep src for any remaining TODO/FIXME/XXX I might have introduced or left. Let me check.\n\n**Risk 3: The `lower-band-v1` prompt content under the `smooth_turn_compression` key.** The smooth_turn_compression derivation uses the lower-band-v1 prompt (placeholder content). Story 3 replaces it with the tested prompt. This is a known placeholder. For Story 0, the derivation produces SOMETHING (the placeholder prompt's output). Not a gap — Story 0 doesn't tune prompts.\n\n**Risk 4: Handoff to Story 1-5.** Let me verify the foundation actually supports downstream stories:\n- Story 1 (smoothed-prompt guards): needs guards.smoothedPrompt (maxInferenceTokens, suspiciousOutputRatio) — ✓ defined + filled. Needs the smoothed_prompt handler to use them — that's Story 1's work. The guard CONFIG is in place. ✓\n- Story 2 (tool-result classification): needs guards.toolResultSummary.timeoutMs — ✓ defined. The classifier module is Story 2's work. ✓\n- Story 3 (smooth turn compression): needs smooth_turn_compression type (✓), compressSmoothTurn method (✓), guards.smoothTurnCompression.tinyTurnTokens (✓), target ratios on assignment (✓ ModelAssignment). The prompt content (smooth-turn-compression-v1) is Story 3's deliverable. ✓\n- Story 4 (chunk detailed deterministic): chunk_summary_detailed type (✓), deterministic handler is Story 4's work. ✓\n- Story 5 (chunk brief): chunk_summary_brief type (✓), target ratios (✓). Story 5's work. ✓\n\nSo the foundation supports all downstream stories. Good handoff.\n\n**Risk 5: Did the rename leave the `chunk_summary_detailed` / `chunk_summary_brief` derivation types intact?** Yes — those weren't renamed (only lower_band_projection → smooth_turn_compression). ✓\n\n**Risk 6: The `inference-real.test.ts` real suite.** It's gated (OPENROUTER_API_KEY). In CI-default (no key), it emits NOT-RAN + accounting tests pass. My cast fix keeps it compiling. The real round-trip (keyed) would now route smooth_turn_compression (renamed). Since the rename is consistent (assignments use smooth_turn_compression via validAssignments), the keyed path should work. But I can't run it without a key. This is a known limitation (the test-plan says real-inference is gated). Not a Story 0 blocker — the architecture-risk test for \"real-inference silently passes when no